00:11:19.280 --> 00:11:20.770
a cleaner way of doing business,

00:11:20.770 --> 00:11:22.310
like a cleaner future.

00:11:22.310 --> 00:11:25.250
But instead we're seeing the
same old Earth destroying

00:11:25.250 --> 00:11:26.850
great power resource race

00:11:26.850 --> 00:11:29.040
that got us here in the first place.

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:31.100
- You good?
- Yeah, my rant is over,

00:11:31.100 --> 00:11:34.240
I'm sorry, this has
gotten me really worked up

00:11:34.240 --> 00:11:36.650
and I'm ready to chill out now.

00:11:36.650 --> 00:11:38.020
- You need to chill.

00:11:39.350 --> 00:11:42.390
- When I used to imagine
a clean energy future,

00:11:42.390 --> 00:11:44.130
I had this image in my head, right,

00:11:44.130 --> 00:11:48.667
of that moment in every
sci-fi where they go, like,

00:11:48.667 --> 00:11:50.627
"Once we lived in harmony."

00:11:50.627 --> 00:11:52.120
♪ People lived in harmony ♪

00:11:52.120 --> 00:11:56.010
- And there's Wakanda and
there's that one Chobani ad

00:11:56.010 --> 00:11:57.950
where everyone is using technology

00:11:57.950 --> 00:12:00.900
and living in a beautiful farm.

00:12:00.900 --> 00:12:02.420
Record scratch, freeze frame,

00:12:02.420 --> 00:12:04.630
here we are in the real world

00:12:04.630 --> 00:12:07.400
where right now it totally is

00:12:07.400 --> 00:12:09.820
the same old story of exploitation

00:12:09.820 --> 00:12:12.740
and harm to the environment
in different ways

00:12:12.740 --> 00:12:14.710
and harm to people.

00:12:14.710 --> 00:12:16.390
I think one thing that's
important to realize

00:12:16.390 --> 00:12:18.890
is that when we talk
about a cleaner future

00:12:18.890 --> 00:12:22.210
we're specifically talking about less CO2,

00:12:22.210 --> 00:12:23.690
and so though it conjures up

00:12:23.690 --> 00:12:26.720
all of these images of
clean in other ways,

00:12:26.720 --> 00:12:28.570
it's on us to do that also.

00:12:28.570 --> 00:12:29.900
There's nothing about the technology

00:12:29.900 --> 00:12:31.800
that inherently will bring that about.

00:12:31.800 --> 00:12:33.850
- Oh dear, what can we do?

00:12:33.850 --> 00:12:35.270
- We have a few things that we can do.

00:12:35.270 --> 00:12:37.663
The first is we can change
the kinds of batteries

00:12:37.663 --> 00:12:38.970
that we're using.

00:12:38.970 --> 00:12:41.150
There's a lot of development
in new kinds of batteries,

00:12:41.150 --> 00:12:44.310
one of them is a lithium-iron battery,

00:12:44.310 --> 00:12:47.270
not a lithium-ion battery,
there's an extra R in there.

00:12:47.270 --> 00:12:49.120
And that battery uses
different kinds of minerals,

00:12:49.120 --> 00:12:50.810
it doesn't use any cobalt.

00:12:50.810 --> 00:12:52.890
That might help this specific example

00:12:52.890 --> 00:12:55.550
but the reality is that
most of these minerals

00:12:55.550 --> 00:12:57.350
have their own stories of exploitation,

00:12:57.350 --> 00:13:00.740
so it's not...
- It's not great.

00:13:00.740 --> 00:13:03.610
- So here's where I start
to feel pretty optimistic.

00:13:03.610 --> 00:13:07.180
There's a big difference between
fossil fuels and minerals.

00:13:07.180 --> 00:13:10.390
You're right that the difference
between electric vehicles

00:13:10.390 --> 00:13:11.630
and gas combustion vehicles

00:13:11.630 --> 00:13:13.490
is that one uses fossil fuels

00:13:13.490 --> 00:13:15.950
and the other we're swapping
up fossil fuels for minerals,

00:13:15.950 --> 00:13:16.880
but there's a big difference

00:13:16.880 --> 00:13:18.280
between fossil fuels and minerals,

00:13:18.280 --> 00:13:21.140
namely fossil fuels are being incinerated

00:13:21.140 --> 00:13:22.460
every time that we drive.

00:13:22.460 --> 00:13:25.000
If we're using more
minerals to power our cars,

00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:26.220
we also have the opportunity

00:13:26.220 --> 00:13:28.200
to get a lot better at recycling.

00:13:28.200 --> 00:13:30.600
This shows how much we already recycle.

00:13:30.600 --> 00:13:33.050
This is end-of-life recycling
rates for select metals

00:13:33.050 --> 00:13:34.190
in electric car batteries.

00:13:34.190 --> 00:13:36.580
- [Johnny] Hm, oh, so
we're already recycling

00:13:36.580 --> 00:13:38.113
a lot of these materials,
including cobalt,

00:13:38.113 --> 00:13:39.840
like it's almost 40%.

00:13:39.840 --> 00:13:41.970
- Yes, and the other thing
to keep in mind here is

00:13:41.970 --> 00:13:44.870
you don't need to recycle
these minerals and metals

00:13:44.870 --> 00:13:46.780
in cars themselves.

00:13:46.780 --> 00:13:48.810
You can have a clean energy future

00:13:48.810 --> 00:13:51.570
where we're recycling metals and minerals

00:13:51.570 --> 00:13:54.600
from batteries that store
wind and solar energy

00:13:54.600 --> 00:13:56.060
into electric cars

00:13:56.060 --> 00:13:57.950
that are then re-recycled back out

00:13:57.950 --> 00:14:00.640
into other forms of storage and batteries.

00:14:00.640 --> 00:14:02.680
- Interesting, okay, wow, yeah,

00:14:02.680 --> 00:14:05.720
that is like an optimistic
view of batteries

00:14:05.720 --> 00:14:07.270
'cause it just feels so finite

00:14:07.270 --> 00:14:10.990
but if the lifespan is
dramatically higher,

00:14:10.990 --> 00:14:12.830
that becomes a much more useful

00:14:12.830 --> 00:14:15.910
and less resource-intensive proposition.

00:14:15.910 --> 00:14:17.560
- There's also one more possibility,

00:14:17.560 --> 00:14:18.890
and I think the people will call me

00:14:18.890 --> 00:14:20.370
a little bit high in the sky here

00:14:20.370 --> 00:14:21.373
but I'm okay with that.

00:14:21.373 --> 00:14:24.120
I think it's possible
that it may not just be

00:14:24.120 --> 00:14:26.120
about fixing the problem with cars,

00:14:26.120 --> 00:14:28.950
but also about fixing the problem of cars.

00:14:28.950 --> 00:14:31.050
There's a high-tech version of this

00:14:31.050 --> 00:14:32.570
where self-driving cars,

00:14:32.570 --> 00:14:34.860
there's some research and speculation

00:14:34.860 --> 00:14:36.960
that they might reduce the amount of cars

00:14:36.960 --> 00:14:38.080
that we're using in total.

00:14:38.080 --> 00:14:39.820
There's also a lower-tech version of this

00:14:39.820 --> 00:14:43.320
where we just invest
more in public transport.

00:14:43.320 --> 00:14:47.390
That might sound unsexy but
it really does seem to me

00:14:47.390 --> 00:14:50.170
like the most promising option.

00:14:50.170 --> 00:14:52.600
I think also if you zoom out for a second

00:14:52.600 --> 00:14:55.620
and think about why we
care about climate change,

00:14:55.620 --> 00:14:58.210
yes, we care about animals

00:14:58.210 --> 00:15:00.850
and we care about the planet itself,

00:15:00.850 --> 00:15:03.170
but we also care a lot
about human suffering.

00:15:03.170 --> 00:15:04.510
One of the reasons why so many people

00:15:04.510 --> 00:15:05.420
care about climate change

00:15:05.420 --> 00:15:07.550
is because it will cause human suffering,

00:15:07.550 --> 00:15:08.780
it already is.

00:15:08.780 --> 00:15:10.240
And so if you take this story

00:15:10.240 --> 00:15:12.870
and you look at the human
suffering caused by mining

00:15:12.870 --> 00:15:13.990
and you then say, oh,

00:15:13.990 --> 00:15:15.520
we're not gonna try
and solve this problem,

00:15:15.520 --> 00:15:18.420
we're just, what, gonna let
more climate change happen?

00:15:18.420 --> 00:15:20.070
We have to solve these
things at the same time

00:15:20.070 --> 00:15:25.010
because both will hurt
more people, basically.

00:15:25.010 --> 00:15:26.550
- Yeah, that's a really good point.

00:15:26.550 --> 00:15:29.700
I think if there's two major takeaways

00:15:29.700 --> 00:15:32.870
that I take from your research into this

00:15:32.870 --> 00:15:35.230
is like, number one,

00:15:35.230 --> 00:15:38.750
a greener future is not inherently

00:15:38.750 --> 00:15:41.233
a cleaner, more idealistic, happy future,

00:15:41.233 --> 00:15:43.560
it's just a less CO2 future,

00:15:43.560 --> 00:15:46.540
which is like, again, obvious,

00:15:46.540 --> 00:15:48.470
it's so obvious when you say it like that

00:15:48.470 --> 00:15:49.410
and make that differentiation,

00:15:49.410 --> 00:15:52.840
but I had those so deeply bound together.

00:15:52.840 --> 00:15:54.880
But I think the more important idea

00:15:54.880 --> 00:15:56.780
is like even though I
have a pessimistic view

00:15:56.780 --> 00:15:59.750
of great power rivalry,

00:15:59.750 --> 00:16:01.100
there's not an option here.

00:16:01.100 --> 00:16:04.560
Like, there's no scenario in
which throwing up my hands

00:16:04.560 --> 00:16:06.770
and being like the world is effed

00:16:06.770 --> 00:16:08.850
is gonna do anything.

00:16:08.850 --> 00:16:12.370
And in fact it's the easier
way out in a lot of ways.

00:16:12.370 --> 00:16:15.970
And so it's good to know that
there is real work being done

00:16:15.970 --> 00:16:17.575
to try to solve this

00:16:17.575 --> 00:16:20.760
and that it's not just bound
to go down the same route.

00:16:20.760 --> 00:16:23.970
- In my mind, this story
isn't a cleaner future

00:16:23.970 --> 00:16:25.620
is just as bad.

00:16:25.620 --> 00:16:29.670
It's a cleaner future is a cleaner future,

00:16:29.670 --> 00:16:31.770
with problems that we still have to solve.

00:16:32.743 --> 00:16:35.326
(gentle music)

